 final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "Looking at the examples, I need to understand the grid structure and transformation rule.\n\nThe grid is divided into cells by a separator color (like 8, 1, or 4). Each cell is 2x2. When there are two or more instances of the same color in the same row or column of cells, the gap between them gets filled with that color.\n\nMore specifically: For each color that appears multiple times, if two instances share the same row of cells, fill all cells between them (horizontally) with that color. If two instances share the same column of cells, fill all cells between them (vertically) with that color.\n\nLet me verify: In Example 1, color 2 appears at cell-row 1, cell-cols 1 and 5. The cells between (cols 2,3,4) get filled with 2. Color 3 at cell-row 4, cell-cols 1 and 3 \u2192 cells between (col 2) filled with 3. Color 2 also appears at cell-rows 1,3 in cell-col 5 \u2192 cells between (row 2) filled with 2.\n\nThis confirms: for each pair of same-colored cells in the same row or column, fill the cells between them with that color.\n\n```python\ndef solve(grid):\n    import copy\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    \n    # Find separator color (most frequent)\n    sep = grid[2][0]  # The separator row\n    \n    # Cell size is 2, separator width is 1, period is 3\n    # Cell positions: cell (r,c) starts at pixel (r*3, c*3) and is 2x2\n    \n    n_cell_rows = (rows + 1) // 3\n    n_cell_cols = (cols + 1) // 3\n    \n    # Read cell values\n    cell_vals = {}\n    for cr in range(n_cell_rows):\n        for cc in range(n_cell_cols):\n            pr, pc = cr * 3, cc * 3\n            val = grid[pr][pc]\n            if val != 0 and val != sep:\n                cell_vals[(cr, cc)] = val\n    \n    # Group by color\n    from collections import defaultdict\n    color_cells = defaultdict(list)\n    for (cr, cc), val in cell_vals.items():\n        color_cells[val].append((cr, cc))\n    \n    result = copy.deepcopy(grid)\n    \n    # For each color, find pairs in same row or column and fill between\n    for color, cells in color_cells.items():\n        # Same row pairs\n        row_groups = defaultdict(list)\n        for cr, cc in cells:\n            row_groups[cr].append(cc)\n        for cr, ccs in row_groups.items():\n            if len(ccs) >= 2:\n                min_cc = min(ccs)\n                max_cc = max(ccs)\n                for cc in range(min_cc, max_cc + 1):\n                    pr, pc = cr * 3, cc * 3\n                    for dr in range(2):\n                        for dc in range(2):\n                            if pr + dr < rows and pc + dc < cols:\n                                if result[pr + dr][pc + dc] == 0:\n                                    result[pr + dr][pc + dc] = color\n        \n        # Same column pairs\n        col_groups = defaultdict(list)\n        for cr, cc in cells:\n            col_groups[cc].append(cr)\n        for cc, crs in col_groups.items():\n            if len(crs) >= 2:\n                min_cr = min(crs)\n                max_cr = max(crs)\n                for cr in range(min_cr, max_cr + 1):\n                    pr, pc = cr * 3, cc * 3\n                    for dr in range(2):\n                        for dc in range(2):\n                            if pr + dr < rows and pc + dc < cols:\n                                if result[pr + dr][pc + dc] == 0:\n                                    result[pr + dr][pc + dc] = color\n    \n    return result\n```",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    import copy\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    \n    # Find separator color (most frequent)\n    sep = grid[2][0]  # The separator row\n    \n    # Cell size is 2, separator width is 1, period is 3\n    # Cell positions: cell (r,c) starts at pixel (r*3, c*3) and is 2x2\n    \n    n_cell_rows = (rows + 1) // 3\n    n_cell_cols = (cols + 1) // 3\n    \n    # Read cell values\n    cell_vals = {}\n    for cr in range(n_cell_rows):\n        for cc in range(n_cell_cols):\n            pr, pc = cr * 3, cc * 3\n            val = grid[pr][pc]\n            if val != 0 and val != sep:\n                cell_vals[(cr, cc)] = val\n    \n    # Group by color\n    from collections import defaultdict\n    color_cells = defaultdict(list)\n    for (cr, cc), val in cell_vals.items():\n        color_cells[val].append((cr, cc))\n    \n    result = copy.deepcopy(grid)\n    \n    # For each color, find pairs in same row or column and fill between\n    for color, cells in color_cells.items():\n        # Same row pairs\n        row_groups = defaultdict(list)\n        for cr, cc in cells:\n            row_groups[cr].append(cc)\n        for cr, ccs in row_groups.items():\n            if len(ccs) >= 2:\n                min_cc = min(ccs)\n                max_cc = max(ccs)\n                for cc in range(min_cc, max_cc + 1):\n                    pr, pc = cr * 3, cc * 3\n                    for dr in range(2):\n                        for dc in range(2):\n                            if pr + dr < rows and pc + dc < cols:\n                                if result[pr + dr][pc + dc] == 0:\n                                    result[pr + dr][pc + dc] = color\n        \n        # Same column pairs\n        col_groups = defaultdict(list)\n        for cr, cc in cells:\n            col_groups[cc].append(cr)\n        for cc, crs in col_groups.items():\n            if len(crs) >= 2:\n                min_cr = min(crs)\n                max_cr = max(crs)\n                for cr in range(min_cr, max_cr + 1):\n                    pr, pc = cr * 3, cc * 3\n                    for dr in range(2):\n                        for dc in range(2):\n                            if pr + dr < rows and pc + dc < cols:\n                                if result[pr + dr][pc + dc] == 0:\n                                    result[pr + dr][pc + dc] = color\n    \n    return result",
